>>__ Parse.temp_set_grammars $ valOf $ grammarDB {thyname="hol"};
## `clasetLib`

``` hol4
structure clasetLib
```

------------------------------------------------------------------------

Maintains the classical rule set used by classical proof tactics.

A classical rule is classified by its use and by whether applying it is
safe for proof search:

| Attribute | Theorem-list marker | Classification |
|---|---|---|
| `[sintro]` | `SIntro th` | safe introduction |
| `[intro]` | `Intro th` | unsafe introduction |
| `[selim]` | `SElim th` | safe elimination |
| `[elim]` | `Elim th` | unsafe elimination |
| `[sdest]` | `SDest th` | safe destruction |
| `[dest]` | `Dest th` | unsafe destruction |
| none | `Del "name"` | remove a rule for one invocation |

Introduction rules match their conclusion against a goal and turn their
premises into subgoals. Elimination rules use a major premise matched
against an assumption. Destruction rules are converted internally to
elimination form. Here *safe* describes proof-search behaviour, not logical
soundness: every rule is a kernel theorem.

### Persistent theorem attributes

The six attributes in the table are argument-free. Plain names denote
unsafe rules; the `s`-prefixed names denote safe rules. A stored declaration
is inherited through theory ancestry:

``` hol4
Theorem conjunction_intro[sintro]:
  p ==> q ==> p /\ q
Proof
  ...
QED
```

The usual `local` theorem modifier makes a declaration local, as in
`[sintro,local]`. Attribute arguments are not accepted. Rule removal is
provided by `delrule` and `temp_delrule`, not by a removal attribute.
Stored rules are normally named by their fully qualified database names,
such as `bool$AND_INTRO_THM`.

### Per-invocation markers

The seven marker constructors modify only the temporary rule set assembled
for a classical tactic call. `SIntro`, `Intro`, `SElim`, `Elim`, `SDest` and
`Dest` add the enclosed theorem with the corresponding classification.
`Del` removes a named rule. The original theorem and global rule set are
unchanged.

An unmarked theorem supplied to a classical or tableau tactic is inserted
into the goal as an assumption. Content-bearing generic simplifier wrappers
such as `Once th` are unwrapped and inserted, while inert generic controls
such as `Cong`, `Excl` and `SF` are ignored. Explicit rule roles therefore
always require one of the six rule markers.

Clasimp theorem lists additionally recognize `Simp th`, which adds only a
temporary rewrite, and `Iff th`, which adds temporary rewrite and classical
views. Classical-only and tableau tactics reject those two markers because
they have no simpset.

### Failure

Malformed rules may be rejected when an attribute or per-invocation marker
is processed. The six rule attributes fail if given arguments.

### Example

The seed conjunction rule can be suppressed for one tactic invocation:

``` hol4
TRY (classicalLib.SAFE_TAC
       [clasetLib.Del "bool$AND_INTRO_THM"])
```

The deletion does not affect later tactic calls.

